+\subsubsection{Action--phase mass--shell route to carrier-clock dilation}
+\label{sec:rt_action_phase_mass_shell_clock}
+
+\paragraph{Purpose and epistemic status.}
+\textbf{[ORTHODOX FORM / BRIDGE / CONDITIONAL DERIVATION].}
+The light-clock construction above derives a Lorentz factor from common signal
+propagation.  This subsection supplies an independent Hamiltonian route for a
+localized carrier with an internal periodic degree of freedom.  The derivation
+is exact once the mass--shell Hamiltonian and a canonical internal action--angle
+pair are admitted.  The unresolved SST step is to derive those structures from
+the substrate rather than import them.
+
+Throughout this subsection, \(H\) denotes the Hamiltonian and
+\(\mathscr H_{\rm hel}\) denotes helicity if needed; the two symbols must not be
+conflated.
+
+\paragraph{Assumptions.}
+Let \(P\) be the conserved translational momentum of an isolated carrier along
+one direction, and let \((I,\theta)\) be an internal action--angle pair.  Assume:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item \(I\) is adiabatically conserved on the translation timescale;
+    \item the positive rest-branch energy is \(E_0(I)>0\);
+    \item the comoving internal frequency is
+    \begin{align}
+        \Omega_0(I)
+        =
+        \frac{dE_0}{dI};
+        \label{eq:rt_action_phase_rest_frequency}
+    \end{align}
+    \item the joint energy satisfies the low-energy mass shell
+    \begin{align}
+        \boxed{
+        H(P,I)
+        =
+        \sqrt{P^2c^2+E_0^2(I)}
+        }.
+        \label{eq:rt_action_phase_mass_shell_hamiltonian}
+    \end{align}
+\end{enumerate}
+The dimensional audit is
+\begin{align}
+    [Pc]=[E_0]=[H]=\mathrm{J},
+    \qquad
+    [I]=\mathrm{J\,s},
+    \qquad
+    [\Omega_0]=\mathrm{s^{-1}}.
+\end{align}
+
+\paragraph{Translational velocity and Lorentz factor.}
+Hamilton's equation gives
+\begin{align}
+    V
+    =
+    \frac{\partial H}{\partial P}
+    =
+    \frac{Pc^2}{H}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_velocity}
+\end{align}
+Consequently,
+\begin{align}
+    1-\frac{V^2}{c^2}
+    &=
+    1-\frac{P^2c^2}{H^2}
+    =
+    \frac{E_0^2}{H^2},
+    \\
+    \frac{E_0}{H}
+    &=
+    \sqrt{1-\frac{V^2}{c^2}}
+    =
+    \frac{1}{\gamma(V)},
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_e0_over_h}
+\end{align}
+with
+\begin{align}
+    H=\gamma E_0,
+    \qquad
+    P=\gamma\frac{E_0}{c^2}V.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_relativistic_relations}
+\end{align}
+
+\paragraph{Internal phase rate.}
+The canonical angle equation must be evaluated at fixed \(P\):
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{d\theta}{dT}
+    =
+    \left.\frac{\partial H}{\partial I}\right|_P
+    =
+    \frac{E_0}{H}\frac{dE_0}{dI}
+    =
+    \frac{\Omega_0}{\gamma(V)}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_clock_rate}
+\end{align}
+If \(d\theta=\Omega_0\,d\tau_A\), then
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \frac{d\tau_A}{dT}
+    =
+    \frac{1}{\gamma(V)}
+    =
+    \sqrt{1-\frac{V^2}{c^2}}
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_proper_time}
+\end{align}
+Thus the internal dynamics remain normal in \(\tau_A\) while their phase
+accumulation is slower relative to the comparison time \(T\).
+
+\textbf{[FIXED-MOMENTUM GUARD].}
+The derivative in Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_action_phase_clock_rate} is a Hamilton equation
+and is therefore taken at fixed canonical momentum.  Rewriting
+\(H=\gamma(V)E_0(I)\) and differentiating at fixed \(V\) gives
+\(\gamma\Omega_0\), but that derivative is not the canonical angle velocity and
+must not be substituted for Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_action_phase_clock_rate}.
+
+\paragraph{Low-momentum inertia check.}
+For \(Pc/E_0\ll1\),
+\begin{align}
+    H(P,I)
+    =
+    E_0(I)
+    +
+    \frac{P^2c^2}{2E_0(I)}
+    -
+    \frac{P^4c^4}{8E_0^3(I)}
+    +
+    O(P^6).
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_low_p_expansion}
+\end{align}
+Writing
+\begin{align}
+    M_0(I)=\frac{E_0(I)}{c^2}
+\end{align}
+gives the Newtonian term \(P^2/(2M_0)\).  This is the same factor-one
+normalization required by the core--torsion inertia gate; an inertia
+\(2E_0/c^2\) would fail this expansion.
+
+\paragraph{Compton specialization.}
+For the calibrated electron branch,
+\begin{align}
+    E_0(I_e)=m_ec^2,
+    \qquad
+    \Omega_0(I_e)=\omega_c
+    =7.76344066\times10^{20}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},
+\end{align}
+so
+\begin{align}
+    \Omega_e(V)
+    =
+    \omega_c\sqrt{1-\frac{V^2}{c^2}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_compton_frequency}
+\end{align}
+Illustrative values are
+\begin{center}
+\begin{tabular}{ccc}
+\hline
+\(V/c\) & \(\gamma^{-1}\) & \(\Omega_e(V)\,[\mathrm{s^{-1}}]\) \\
+\hline
+\(0\) & \(1\) & \(7.76344066\times10^{20}\) \\
+\(0.9\) & \(0.435889894\) & \(3.38400533\times10^{20}\) \\
+\(0.99\) & \(0.141067360\) & \(1.09516808\times10^{20}\) \\
+\(0.999\) & \(0.044710178\) & \(3.47104812\times10^{19}\) \\
+\hline
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+The action normalization
+\begin{align}
+    I_e
+    =
+    \frac{E_0}{\omega_c}
+    =
+    \hbar
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_ie_hbar}
+\end{align}
+is a calibrated identity under the adopted Compton closure, not an independent
+hydrodynamic derivation of \(\hbar\).
+
+\paragraph{Worldline-phase cross-check.}
+For the plane-wave phase convention
+\begin{align}
+    \Phi(X,T)
+    =
+    \frac{PX-HT}{\hbar},
+\end{align}
+and a carrier trajectory \(dX/dT=V\),
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{d\Phi}{dT}
+    &=
+    \frac{PV-H}{\hbar}
+    =
+    -\frac{E_0^2}{\hbar H}
+    =
+    -\frac{E_0}{\hbar\gamma}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_worldline_phase}
+\end{align}
+The magnitude of the phase rate along the carrier therefore decreases as
+\(1/\gamma\).  By contrast, the coordinate-frequency coefficient at fixed
+position is \(H/\hbar=\gamma E_0/\hbar\).  These are different derivatives and
+must not be conflated \cite{DeBroglie1925}.
+
+\paragraph{No flattening or stretching follows from the mass shell.}
+Introduce internal shape coordinates \(q^a\) and let
+\begin{align}
+    H(P,I,q)
+    =
+    \sqrt{P^2c^2+E_0^2(I,q)}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_shape_hamiltonian}
+\end{align}
+Then
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{\partial H}{\partial q^a}
+    =
+    \frac{E_0}{H}
+    \frac{\partial E_0}{\partial q^a}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_shape_gradient}
+\end{align}
+For \(E_0>0\),
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{\partial H}{\partial q^a}=0
+    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+    \frac{\partial E_0}{\partial q^a}=0.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_same_shape_extrema}
+\end{align}
+At such an extremum,
+\begin{align}
+    \left.\frac{\partial^2H}{\partial q^a\partial q^b}\right|_*
+    =
+    \frac{E_0}{H}
+    \left.\frac{\partial^2E_0}{\partial q^a\partial q^b}\right|_*.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_shape_hessian}
+\end{align}
+The positive factor \(E_0/H\) preserves the Hessian signature.  Therefore a
+rest-shape minimum remains a minimum for all finite \(P\) in this separable
+mass--shell bridge.  Translation-induced flattening, stretching, or uniform
+core thinning would require an additional explicit coupling such as
+\(H_{Pq}(P,I,q)\); none is assumed in v0.8.28.
+
+\paragraph{Residuals for an SST derivation.}
+A numerical or analytic carrier family must report
+\begin{align}
+    \Delta_{\rm shell}
+    &:=\frac{H^2-P^2c^2-E_0^2}{E_0^2},
+    \\
+    \Delta_V
+    &:=\frac{VH}{Pc^2}-1,
+    \\
+    \Delta_\Omega
+    &:=\frac{\Omega(P)H}{\Omega_0E_0}-1,
+    \\
+    \Delta_q(P)
+    &:=\frac{\lVert q_*(P)-q_*(0)\rVert}{q_{\rm ref}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_action_phase_residual_vector}
+\end{align}
+The first three residuals must converge to zero under domain, core-profile, and
+resolution refinement.  Under the separable bridge, \(\Delta_q(P)\) must also
+converge to zero after removing rigid translation, rotation, and
+reparametrization.  A reproducible nonzero \(\Delta_q\) is evidence for an
+additional momentum--shape coupling, not evidence supplied by the mass shell
+itself.
+
+\paragraph{Minimal computation programme.}
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item Identify a stable rest carrier and a concrete internal phase \(\theta\).
+    \item Construct fixed-\(P\), fixed-\(I\) translating solutions or constrained
+    minimizers and measure \(H(P,I)\), \(V(P,I)\), and \(\Omega(P,I)\).
+    \item Test Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_action_phase_residual_vector} over at least one
+    decade of small \(P/E_0c\) and into the relativistic regime allowed by the
+    numerical model.
+    \item Repeat for multiple knot species and orientations to test monometricity.
+    \item Reject the bridge if a non-removable species-dependent limiting speed,
+    a non-convergent mass-shell residual, or an internal phase ratio different
+    from \(E_0/H\) survives refinement.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\textbf{[OPEN THEOREM TARGET].}
+Derive Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_action_phase_mass_shell_hamiltonian} from the microscopic
+SST action, rather than assuming the orthodox mass shell; identify the carrier
+action \(I\) from circulation, phase, or a reduced symplectic form; and prove
+that the same \(c\) appears for every stable low-energy carrier
+\cite{Einstein1905,Arnold1989ClassicalMechanics}.
